[1490.062778,"o","\u001b[?25l\u001b[H  Note this isn't the differential-censoring problem I checked earlier and cleared \u2014 median follow-up was\u001b[2;1H  near-identical (9.92 vs 9.88 months). The asymmetry here is a \u001b[3mconsequence\u001b[m\u000f of the treatment effect: the\u001b[K\u001b[3;2H\u001b[1K\u001b[Carm that dies faster accumulates less observed time. Kaplan-Meier handles that automatically; dividing\u001b[K\r\n  by 714 does not.\u001b[K\r\n  \u001b[K\r\n  \u001b[1mThe general rule\u001b[m\u000f\u001b[K\r\n  \u001b[K\r\n  Naive counting is unbiased only when follow-up is complete. Here it's 48% incomplete, and the error is\u001b[K\r\n  almost exactly that: censor half your cohort, inflate survival by roughly half the true mortality you\u001b[K\u001b[10;2H\u001b[1K\u001b[Cfailed to observe.\u001b[K\r\n  \u001b[K\r\n  The one thing that would break KM too: if censoring were \u001b[3minformative\u001b[m\u000f \u2014 patients withdrawn because they\u001b[K\u001b[13;2H\u001b[1K\u001b[Cwere dying. The design earns some reassurance that it isn't, since censoring here is dominated by\u001b[K\r\n  administrative cutoff (enrolment 2018\u20132019, export at ~24 months) rather than by dropout. But these\u001b[K\r"]
